This issue might be due to the fact that SvelteKit uses server-side rendering (SSR) by default. When a note is deleted, the server-side state is updated, but the client-side state is not updated immediately. This is because the client-side state is separate from the server-side state.

To resolve this issue, you can use the `serverUpdate` hook in SvelteKit to update the client-side state when the server-side state changes. Here's an example of how you can do this:

```javascript
import { action } from'sveltekit';
import { update } from'sveltekit';

const deleteNote = action('deleteNote');

const Notes = () => {
  const [notes, setNotes] = useState([]);

  useEffect(() => {
    const serverNotes = await update('notes');
    setNotes(serverNotes);
  }, []);

  const serverUpdate = async (data) => {
    const serverNotes = await update('notes');
    setNotes(serverNotes);
  };

  return (
    <div>
      {notes.map(note => (
        <div key={note.id}>
          <p>{note.content}</p>
          <button onClick={() => deleteNote(note.id)}>Delete</button>
        </div>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
};

export default Notes;
```

In this example, the `serverUpdate` hook is used to update the clientnotes state when the server-side state changes. The `serverUpdate` hook is called when the `deleteNote` action is triggered, which updates the server-side state. The `serverUpdate` hook then updates the client-side state with the new server-side state.

Note that the `serverUpdate` hook is called with the `data` parameter, which is the data that was passed to the server-side action. In this case, the `data` parameter is not used, but you can use it if you need to.

