Here is a JavaScript code that illustrates how to use a strategy pattern in a banking app system:

```javascript
// Define the strategy interface
class Strategy {
  calculate() {
    throw new Error("The calculate method must be implemented by the strategy");
  }
}

// Define the concrete strategies
class SimpleStrategy extends Strategy {
  calculate() {
    return 0.01 * this.accountBalance;
  }
}

class ComplexStrategy extends Strategy {
  calculate() {
    return 0.01 * this.accountBalance + 0.02 * this.accountBalance / 2;
  }
}

// Define the context class
class Account {
  constructor(accountBalance, strategy) {
    this.accountBalance = accountBalance;
    this.strategy = strategy;
  }

  calculateInterest() {
    return this.strategy.calculate();
  }
}

// Use the strategy pattern in a banking app
const account1 = new Account(1000, new SimpleStrategy());
const account2 = new Account(1000, new ComplexStrategy());

console.log(account1.calculateInterest()); // 10
console.log(account2.calculateInterest()); // 11
```

In this code, we define a strategy interface `Strategy` and two concrete strategies `SimpleStrategy` and `ComplexStrategy`. The `Strategy` class has a `calculate` method that must be implemented by the concrete strategies. The `Account` class is the context class that takes an account balance and a strategy as parameters. The `calculateInterest` method calls the `calculate` method of the strategy to calculate the interest.

